<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John of Ibelin, Count of Jaffa and Ascalon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1215–1266</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as John of Jaffa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John of Ibelin, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a>, was a nephew of the Old Lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a> and the leading jurist of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>. His treatise on the laws and customs of the High Court, part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assizes-of-jerusalem">Assizes of Jerusalem</a>, is the largest legal work of the crusader states. He fought with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a> at Damietta in 1249 and strengthened the defences of Jaffa.</p></div>
